    "question": {
      ":question/id": "890c0660-546a-581b-8c7c-4a4460a7717d",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Complete the square on $x^2-7x+\\frac{49}{4}$.\n\nFill in the nonnegative numbers.\n\n$x^2-7x+\\frac{49}{4}=(x-$ {{field-1}} $)^2+$ {{field-2}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The half of the linear coefficient is $-\\frac{7}{2}$. **Add and subtract** its square so the expression keeps its value.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^2-7x+\\frac{49}{4}&=\\underbrace{x^2-2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}x+\\frac{49}{4}}_{\\text{perfect square}}-\\frac{49}{4}+\\frac{49}{4} \\\\\n&=\\left(x-\\frac{7}{2}\\right)^2-\\frac{49}{4}+\\frac{49}{4} \\\\\n&=\\left(x-\\frac{7}{2}\\right)^2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe minus sign inside the square produces the required linear term $-7x$.\n\nThe blanks are $\\frac{7}{2}$ and $0$; the sign before the second blank is already supplied.",
